## Visible pre-order record

[History]
Mr. ___ is a ___ gentleman with COPD (stage IV with  FEV1 22%, on 5L O2), pulmonary hypertension, non-ischemic CM (EF  30% in ___, paroxysmal AFib on Warfarin, VT s/p ICD with  frequent ectopy, and adrenal insufficiency who presented to the  ED with abdominal pain and is admitted to the MICU due to COPD  exacerbation with need for BiPAP.  For the past 1 month he has complained of abdomenal pain in his  RUQ>RLQ.  It seems to be worse after eating, associated with  bloating and nausea.  The pain became worse last night, so he  did not sleep well.  In the morning, his son picked him up to go  to a Pulmonology appointment but found him to be exhausted and  in pain so decided to take him to the ED instead.  In the ___ ED, initial VS were: pain ___, T 97.3, HR 68, BP  143/99, RR 18, POx 90% 5L NC.  HR increased to 120-130 for the  rest of his ED stay; he was in AFib with EKG similar to prior.   Abdominal exam was benign.  Labs were notable for WBC 15.9  (87.8% PMN, no bands), Cr 1.2 (recent baseline is 1.1), lactate  2.2, INR 3.7.  CT abdomen/pelvis with contrast was obtained due  to his abdominal pain and was notable for cholelithiasis in a  distended gallbladder.  During his workup, he was briefly off O2  and desatted to 85%RA which did not improve when supplemental O2  was administered, so he was placed on BiPAP.  O2 sat improved to  the mid/high 90's.  CXR showed no acute cardiac or pulmonary  process.  He was treated for COPD exacerbation with  Methylprednisolone 125mg IV, Albuterol/Ipratropium nebs, and  Levofloxacin IV but during Abx infusion he developed arm itching  so infusion was stopped and he was given Diphenhydramine 25mg  IV.  EKG was not concerning for ischemia and troponin 0.03; he  received ASA 81mg x4.  In addition, for anxiety while on BiPAP  he was given Lorazepam 0.5mg IV.  After an hour of BiPAP, an  attempt was made to wean off but this was unsuccessful so he was  admitted to the MICU.  VS prior to transfer were: T97.4, HR 121,  BP 116/55, RR 24, POx 99% on BiPAP with PS 8, PEEP 5, FiO2 40%.  On arrival to the MICU, he was taken off BiPAP and placed on 5L  NC.  He is sleepy but arousable.  He does not answer questions,  but grimaces when his abdomen is palpated.    Review of systems:   Unable to answer   Past Medical History: #. severe COPD -stage 4, FEV1 22% of predicted -on DuoNebs QID, Spiriva, Advair and Combivent MDI PRN -failed roflumilast #. moderate pulmonary arterial hypertension -___ RHC with minimally elevated right-sided filling pressure,  minimally elevated PA, pulmonary artery diastolic pressure, and  moderate pulmonary artery hypertension #. AFib on Warfarin #. class III CHF from non-ischemic CM, EF 30% #. VT s/p dual-chamber ICD ___ #. ventricular ectopy #. adrenal insufficiency #. HLD #. GERD #. gout #. h/o bladder cancer   Social History: ___ Family History: Nobody in the family with lung problems.

[Physical examination]
ADMISSION EXAM Vitals: [captured_vital], [captured_vital], [captured_vital], [captured_vital], [captured_vital] 5L NC General: Alert, oriented, no acute distress   HEENT: Sclera anicteric, MMM, oropharynx clear, EOMI, PERRL   Neck: supple, JVP not elevated, no LAD   CV: Regular rate and rhythm, normal S1 + S2, no murmurs, rubs,  gallops   Lungs: Clear to auscultation anteriorly Abdomen: mildly distended, TTP in RUQ with guarding, no rebound  tenderness, no palpable mass Ext: warm, well perfused, 2+ pulses, no clubbing, cyanosis or  edema  Neuro: somnolent but arousable to voice

[Prior resulted imaging]
[Prior imaging 1: Radiograph Chest (CHEST (PA & LAT))]
FINDINGS:
Frontal and lateral radiographs of the chest were acquired.  There
is redemonstration of a left-sided pacemaker with associated right atrial and
right ventricular leads, appropriately positioned.  The lungs are clear.  The
heart size is within normal limits.  The mediastinal contours are normal. 
There are no pleural effusions.  No pneumothorax is seen.

[Prior imaging 2: CT Abdomen (CT ABD & PELVIS WITH CONTRAST)]
TECHNIQUE:
MDCT axial images were acquired through the abdomen and pelvis
following the administration of intravenous contrast material only. 
Multiplanar reformats were performed.

ABDOMEN CT:
There are incompletely imaged pacemaker leads ending within the
right atrium and ventricle.  The visualized portions of the lung bases are
clear.  The liver is unremarkable.  There is no intrahepatic biliary duct
dilatation.  The portal vein is patent.  Multiple tiny stones are seen
layering within the dependent portion of the gallbladder.  There is no
gallbladder wall thickening.  There may be minimal pericholecystic fat
stranding along the fundus of the gallbladder (2:36).  The spleen is
unremarkable.  The pancreas is unremarkable.  The adrenal glands are normal. 
A 4.7 cm exophytic cyst extends from the left lower renal pole, increased in
size compared to the prior CT from ___, previously measuring up
to 2.8 cm.  Additional tiny bilateral renal hypodensities are too small to
characterize but are statistically simple cysts.  The kidneys are otherwise
remarkable.  There is colonic diverticulosis without evidence of
diverticulitis.  The colon is otherwise normal.  The appendix is not
definitely identified although there is no fat stranding in the expected
region of the appendix to suggest acute appendicitis.  There is no free fluid
or free air in the abdomen.  There is an infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm
measuring up to 3.7 cm, not significantly changed.  Aneurysmal dilatation of
the right common iliac artery is also not significantly changed, measuring up
to 2 cm.  There are no pathologically enlarged lymph nodes in the abdomen.

PELVIS CT:
The bladder is unremarkable.  There is no free fluid in the
pelvis.  No pathologically enlarged pelvic lymph nodes are seen.  A large
uncomplicated fat-containing right inguinal hernia is increased in size
compared to the prior study from ___.

BONE WINDOW:
No suspicious lytic or blastic lesions are identified. 
Multilevel degenerative changes of the thoracolumbar spine are noted.

